  <h2>Therapy that does not look like therapy</h2>
  <p>Ask a nine-year-old how they are feeling and you will get "fine", or a shrug, or a question about whether this is going to take long. It is not evasion. Most children do not have the vocabulary yet for what is happening inside them, and being asked to produce it on demand is its own small pressure.</p>
  <p>So with younger clients, Austin works sideways. Art, music, movement, and games carry the session, and the real material arrives through them: who the character in the drawing is afraid of, what happens in the game when someone breaks a rule, how a body moves when it is angry versus when it is worried. A child will tell you the truth through a made-up story long before they will tell you straight out.</p>
  <p>With teenagers it looks more conventional, two people talking, but the same principle holds. Nobody is graded on how well they do therapy. If a session goes twenty minutes on a game they are into before it goes anywhere near the hard thing, that is not wasted time. That is how the hard thing becomes sayable.</p>
  <h2>The Men's Support Group</h2>
  <p>Austin also leads the practice's Men's Support Group. It exists for a simple reason: a lot of men have exactly one person they talk to honestly, and sometimes not even that. A group is a different instrument than individual therapy. Hearing someone else say the thing you assumed was yours alone does something a room with one other person in it cannot quite manage.</p>
  <p>If you are curious whether it would suit you, the free call is the place to ask.</p>
